    "created_at": "2016-10-10T17:56:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-10-10T17:56:05Z",
    "body": "The problem is that it's not \"some inconsistency now\", it's _guaranteed_ inconsistency until we actually get the new primitive this wants to rely on.  This sort of \"eh, go ahead and do something that kinda works\" is why we have several APIs from the brief period between \"promises are cool\" and \"here's promises\" which have weird, inconsistent callback APIs. The convenience gain from switching to bespoke callbacks was very small, compared to the gain from actual promises; it would have been better for those to have stuck with events, which can be upgraded into promises in a consistent way.\n\nSame here.  This wants to send the current value when you subscribe (like a fulfilled promise does) but also wants to send updates (which events do). The correct primitive is an Observable, but we haven't gotten that finished up for the ES spec yet. It's better, as Domenic says, to just apply existing platform patterns to this API for now, rather than inventing something bespoke (that's still inadequate, when compared to the \"correct\" solution) which'll just look like an awkward one-off in a few years.\n",
